{"name":"Sapphire Earrings","hex":"#284b75","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sapphire","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A clear deep blue variety of corundum, valued as a precious stone.","A white, yellow, or purple variety of corundum, either clear or translucent."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["of a deep blue colour.","pertaining to a 45th year"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sapphire"},{"term":"earrings","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of earring"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/earrings"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sapphire","blocks":[{"text":"Sapphire is a gemstone variety of the mineral corundum, the crystalline form of aluminium oxide (α-Al2O3). Pure corundum is colorless, while trace elements in its crystal structure produce a wide range of colors. Blue is the best-known, but sapphires also occur in yellow, green, purple, pink, orange, gray, black, and colorless forms. Red gem-quality corundum is classified as ruby rather than sapphire. The boundary between ruby and pink sapphire is based on color appearance and varies among gemological and trade conventions. Sapphire has a Mohs hardness of 9, making it highly resistant to scratching and abrasion."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Logan_Sapphire_10956420_cropped.png/330px-Logan_Sapphire_10956420_cropped.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sapphire"},{"title":"Earring","blocks":[{"text":"Earrings are jewelry that are worn on one's ears. Earrings are often worn through earlobe piercings or another external part of the ear, or by some other means, such as stickers or clip-ons. Earrings have been worn across multiple civilizations and historic periods, often carrying a cultural significance."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Positions_of_earrings.jpg/330px-Positions_of_earrings.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earring"},{"title":"Shades of blue","blocks":[{"text":"Varieties of the color blue may differ in hue, chroma, or lightness, or in two or three of these qualities. Variations in value are also called tints and shades, a tint being a blue or other hue mixed with white, a shade being mixed with black. A large selection of these colors is shown below."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Shades_of_Blue.svg/330px-Shades_of_Blue.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Alkaline_pegmatite.jpg/500px-Alkaline_pegmatite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Alkaline_pegmatite.jpg/1280px-Alkaline_pegmatite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Alkaline_pegmatite.jpg/500px-Alkaline_pegmatite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Blue corundum crystals in weathered feldspar in an alkaline pegmatite from the Canaã massif, Brazil.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlkaline_pegmatite.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Sapphire_%28GeoDIL_number_-_1432%29.jpg/500px-Sapphire_%28GeoDIL_number_-_1432%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Sapphire_%28GeoDIL_number_-_1432%29.jpg/1280px-Sapphire_%28GeoDIL_number_-_1432%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Sapphire_%28GeoDIL_number_-_1432